<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salvador Correia de Sá e Benevides</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1602–1688</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Salvador Correia de Sá</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> Correia de Sá e Benevides was a Portuguese-Brazilian soldier and administrator from a leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a> family, several times governor of Rio. In 1648 he led a fleet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> that retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>, restoring Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> that supplied Brazil's plantations. He then governed Angola until 1651.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salvador-correia-de-sa-e-benevides.jpg" alt="Salvador Correia de Sá e Benevides" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Feliciano de Almeida (1635-1694) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salvador_Correia_de_S%C3%A1_e_Benevides_(1602-1688),_1673-1675_-_Feliciano_de_Almeida_(Galleria_degli_Uffizi,_Florence).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
